<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Before the Tour de France, cycle racing was a raw experiment with primitive roads, six-day races, motor pacing and riders at their limits."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/exhausted-rider.webp"/><title>When cycle racing was the Wild West · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/cykelsportens-vilde-vesten/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero"><div><p class="section-label">Story · The pioneering years</p><h1>When cycle racing was the <em>Wild West</em> on two wheels</h1></div><p>Before the Tour de France gathered the heroes and scandals into one enormous race, there was already a raw world of endurance, risk and technical experiments.</p></header>
<figure class="wildwest-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/exhausted-rider.webp`} alt="Exhausted track cyclist on a wooden velodrome"/><figcaption>Track racing turned exhaustion into popular entertainment. The archive does not document the precise date or rider.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead"><p class="section-label">Before the Tour</p><p>The longer the race, the worse the roads and the more exhausted the riders became, the better the story often was for the newspapers and promoters paying for it. Riders fought darkness, lack of sleep, dust, cold and primitive bicycles — as well as rivals who did not always believe the entire route had to be covered by bicycle.</p></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">A new kind of hero</p><h2>From the workshop to the newspapers</h2></div><div><p>At the end of the nineteenth century, the bicycle became a symbol of the modern age. It gave ordinary people mobility that had previously required a horse or a train ticket, and it created a new sporting star: the professional cyclist.</p><p>Many came from working-class backgrounds, where prize money could exceed an ordinary annual wage. Maurice Garin had worked as a chimney sweep and became known as <em>Le Petit Ramoneur</em> — the little chimney sweep. His rise became an image of the tough worker who could ride towards another life.</p><p>Contracts were short, however, and an injury could end a career overnight. Riders therefore accepted risks that would be unthinkable today.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-image-row"><figure><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/bouhours-1900.webp`} alt="Émile Bouhours on a racing bicycle around 1900" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Émile Bouhours, winner of Paris–Roubaix in 1900, on a simple racing bicycle of the period.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/sprint-1900.webp`} alt="Cyclists sprinting around 1900" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A track sprint around 1900. Small pictures like this helped turn riders into recognisable sporting stars.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">The classics emerge</p><h2>Races before the Tour de France</h2></div><div><p>The Tour did not appear from nowhere. Liège–Bastogne–Liège was first held in 1892. Only 17 riders reached the finish, and the last arrived more than five hours after winner Léon Houa.</p><p>The first Paris–Roubaix followed in 1896: roughly 280 kilometres on primitive roads and cobbles. Germany’s Josef Fischer won in 9 hours 17 minutes, with Maurice Garin third. The cobbles were not romantic scenery. Rain filled the joints with mud; in dry weather, dust hung over the peloton.</p><p>The first edition also used pacing. Cyclists — and motorised pacers on parts of the route — could reduce wind resistance for the riders. The boundary between individual performance, teamwork and mechanical assistance had yet to be settled.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Rider and machine</p><h2>One gear for the whole day</h2></div><div><p>A racing bicycle around 1900 had a steel frame, often wooden rims and simple brakes — if it had brakes at all. Many used a fixed hub or a single freewheel and one ratio. The rider chose the gear before the start and hoped it would suit both flat roads and hills.</p><p>Tarmac was rare. Outside towns, roads were earth, gravel and loose stones; after rain they became mud with deep ruts. A puncture was largely the rider’s own problem. At night, a road sign or a local resident might be the only navigation.</p></div></section>
<figure class="wildwest-wide"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/track-racing.webp`} alt="Large field on an outdoor cycle track around 1900" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Track racing before a large crowd around the turn of the century. The velodrome was both sporting arena and modern popular entertainment.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Six days in a wooden bowl</p><h2>Exhaustion as spectacle</h2></div><div><p>Early six-day races were individual endurance contests. At Madison Square Garden, spectators followed the riders around the clock while cigar smoke, dust and heat hung over the wooden track. Every minute away gave rivals a chance to increase their lead.</p><p>After days without sleep, some riders became disoriented, hallucinated or continued almost mechanically. When authorities in New York and Illinois limited each rider to 12 hours a day in 1898, organisers paired them. One could rest while the other rode. This produced the two-rider discipline known as the <em>Madison</em>.</p><p>Motor-paced racing added engine noise and exhaust fumes. Riders stayed close behind large pacing motorcycles and reached otherwise impossible speeds. The crowd enjoyed the combination of speed, mechanics and danger.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">The little black bottle</p><h2>The body simply had to continue</h2></div><div><p>There were no modern anti-doping rules. Caffeine, alcohol, ether, cocaine and small doses of strychnine appear in accounts from the period, though documentation for individual stories is often uncertain. The line between medicine, stimulant and poison was unclear.</p><p>A rider did not always know what a trainer’s secret mixture contained. The Englishman James “Choppy” Warburton became notorious for his little black bottle, but its contents have never been established. Such substances probably did not make the body stronger; they could make warning signs easier to ignore for a little longer.</p><p>Arthur Linton is often described as the sport’s first doping victim, but contemporary information suggests that his death in 1896 was probably caused by illness. The pioneering years are dramatic enough without turning uncertain myths into facts.</p></div></section>
